1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
 Hi, in this lesson, I will talk about custom instructions in GitHub code pilot.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:06,500
 Let's get started.

3
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:12,000
 When you chat with GitHub code pilot, you might often find yourself repeating the same context.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:18,000
 Like, saying, we use TypeScript or use React functional components.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,000
 With custom instructions, you don't have to do that anymore.

6
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,500
 You can save all of these preferences in a Markzone file once.

7
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:30,500
 And code pilot will automatically follow them in every chat request.

8
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:34,500
 This makes your AI assistant feel more personal.

9
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:39,500
 Almost like, it truly understands your project and your unique coding style.

10
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:43,500
 So, what exactly do custom instructions do?

11
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:49,500
 Think of them as a built-in guide that tells code pilot how your project works.

12
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:55,500
 You can define coding rules, naming styles or language preferences, all in one place.

13
00:00:56,500 --> 00:01:03,500
 For example, you could tell code pilot to use camel case for variables, Pascal case for components,

14
00:01:03,500 --> 00:01:09,500
 always wrap a sim code in try and catch blocks, and to write tests for every public function.

15
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:14,500
 Once you've defined these rules, code pilot automatically applies them to its chat responses,

16
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:18,500
 making sure all answers fit your project standards.

17
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:23,500
 Just remember, custom instructions only affect chat responses.

18
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:27,500
 Note the small code suggestions you see as you type.

19
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:29,500
 Now, how do they actually work?

20
00:01:29,500 --> 00:01:34,500
 You simply write your instructions inside Markdown files.

21
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:41,500
 Visual Studio Code reads those files, and includes them as part of Copitis chat context.

22
00:01:41,500 --> 00:01:45,500
 That means you no longer need to explain your setup every time.

23
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:48,500
 Copitis already knows it.

24
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:54,500
 You can make these instructions apply to the whole workspace to specific files or folders,

25
00:01:54,500 --> 00:01:58,500
 or even attach them manually to individual chat prompts.

26
00:01:58,500 --> 00:02:04,500
 So, there are a few different ways to organize your custom instructions in Visual Studio Code.

27
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:07,500
 Let's look into each option separately.

28
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:13,500
 The first option is the file name dot github/copilot-instructions.md.

29
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:17,500
 This is the main file for your entire workspace.

30
00:02:17,500 --> 00:02:23,500
 It sits inside your projects.github folder and applies to every single chat request.

31
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:29,500
 If you just want one simple setup for your whole project, this is the best option to use.

32
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:33,500
 The second option is dot-instructions.md.

33
00:02:33,500 --> 00:02:41,500
 These are smaller, more focused instruction files meant for specific files, folders, or tasks.

34
00:02:41,500 --> 00:02:48,500
 You can create multiple of them, one for React components, one for test files, another for documentation,

35
00:02:48,500 --> 00:02:53,500
 and Visual Studio Code will automatically combine them together.

36
00:02:53,500 --> 00:03:04,500
 You can also narrow them down using a property called apply2, which tell Visual Studio Code exactly which files or folders those instructions should apply to.

37
00:03:04,500 --> 00:03:10,500
 The third type is agents.md, and this one is still experimental.

38
00:03:10,500 --> 00:03:19,500
 It's designed for workspaces that use multiple AI agents, like separate agents for documentation, frontend, and backend.

39
00:03:19,500 --> 00:03:24,500
 This file applies automatically to all chat requests in your workspace.

40
00:03:24,500 --> 00:03:33,500
 You can even enable nested agents.md files, meaning that each folder can have its own specific set of rules.

41
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:41,500
 The wrap-up. Custom instructions let you teach GitHub co-pilot how to work like your team.

42
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:50,500
 They help make a response's more consistent, match your coding style, and save you time by not having to repeat the same rules over and over.

43
00:03:50,500 --> 00:03:58,500
 You can use a single markdown file for your entire project or create multiple smaller ones for different tasks.

44
00:03:58,500 --> 00:04:06,500
 With their setup, co-pilot automatically follows your rules every time you chat, making it feel like a true part of your team.

45
00:04:06,500 --> 00:04:11,500
 I hope this lesson was useful for you and see you in the next lesson.

